# Applications of Calculus: Maxima, Minima, and Areas

> Differentiation measures how fast things change; integration adds the
> changes back up. Together they answer two practical questions better
> than any other tool: what is the best value, and how large the region?

## 1. What the Sign of the Derivative Tells You

| Sign of f′(x) | Behaviour of f | Graph |
| --- | --- | --- |
| positive | increasing | rising left to right |
| negative | decreasing | falling left to right |
| zero at a point | critical point | possible peak, valley, or terrace |

[definition]
A **critical point** is an interior point c where f′(c) = 0 or the
derivative fails to exist. Local maxima and minima can only occur at
critical points — or at the endpoints of a closed interval.
[/definition]

[example]
For f(x) = x², the derivative is f′(x) = 2x: negative for x < 0 and
positive for x > 0. The graph falls into x = 0 and rises out of it, so
the vertex (0, 0) is a local (and global) minimum.
[/example]

## 2. The First Derivative Test

Walk through a critical point and watch the sign of f′ change: positive
to negative marks a **local maximum**; negative to positive marks a
**local minimum**; no sign change means neither — a terrace, like
x³ has at x = 0.

[example]
Find the minimum of f(x) = x² − 8x + 7.
Step 1: f′(x) = 2x − 8, which is zero at x = 4.
Step 2: for x < 4 the derivative is negative (falling); for x > 4 it
is positive (rising).
Step 3: the sign changes from − to +, so x = 4 is a minimum, and
f(4) = 16 − 32 + 7 = −9.
[/example]

## 3. The Second Derivative Test

The second derivative measures concavity — the direction the curve
bends — and sorts critical points without a sign walk:

@math label="eq:second-derivative-test"
f''(c) > 0 \text{ (minimum)} \qquad f''(c) < 0 \text{ (maximum)}
@/math

[example]
Classify the critical points of f(x) = 2x³ − 9x² + 12x.
Step 1: f′(x) = 6x² − 18x + 12 = 6(x − 1)(x − 2), so the critical
points are x = 1 and x = 2.
Step 2: f″(x) = 12x − 18; f″(1) = −6 < 0, so x = 1 is a local maximum
with f(1) = 5.
Step 3: f″(2) = 6 > 0, so x = 2 is a local minimum with f(2) = 4.
[/example]

[important]
The second derivative test is silent when f″(c) = 0 — the point might
be a maximum, a minimum, or neither. When that happens, fall back to
the first derivative test.
[/important]

## 4. Endpoints Matter: Closed Intervals

On a closed interval, the global maximum and minimum may sit at the
ends, where the derivative need not vanish. The recipe: evaluate f at
every critical point and at both endpoints, then compare.

[example]
Find the global extremes of f(x) = x³ − 3x on the interval from −2
to 2.
Step 1: f′(x) = 3x² − 3 = 3(x − 1)(x + 1), so the critical points are
x = −1 and x = 1.
Step 2: evaluate everything: f(−2) = −2, f(−1) = 2, f(1) = −2,
f(2) = 2.
Step 3: the global maximum is 2 (at x = −1 and again at the endpoint
x = 2) and the global minimum is −2 (at x = 1 and x = −2).
[/example]

## 5. Optimisation: The Fencing Problem

@diagram tree id="optimisation-workflow" layout="tree" aria="Workflow for solving optimisation problems with derivatives"
  "Optimisation workflow" -> "1. Name the variables and sketch"
  "Optimisation workflow" -> "2. Write the objective in one variable"
  "Optimisation workflow" -> "3. Differentiate and set derivative to zero"
  "Optimisation workflow" -> "4. Classify with the second derivative"
  "Optimisation workflow" -> "5. Answer in context with units"
@/diagram

[example]
A farmer has 40 m of fence for a rectangular pen. Which dimensions give
the largest area?
Step 1: let one side be x; then the other is 20 − x (the perimeter is
2x + 2y = 40).
Step 2: the area is

@math label="eq:fence-area"
A(x) = x(20 - x) = 20x - x^2
@/math

Step 3: A′(x) = 20 − 2x, which is zero at x = 10.
Step 4: A″(x) = −2 < 0, so x = 10 is a maximum.
Step 5: the best pen is 10 m by 10 m — a square — with area
A(10) = 100 m².
[/example]

[warning]
Never forget the constraint. Writing A(x) = x·y with two unknowns goes
nowhere; use the perimeter condition to eliminate y first. And check
the answer makes physical sense: negative or zero side lengths are not
pens.
[/warning]

The area chart for A(x) = x(20 − x) rises to its peak at x = 10 and
falls symmetrically — the parabola's vertex is exactly what the
derivative found:

@chart line title="Fenced area A = x(20 − x) for 40 m of fence" labels:
0
5
10
15
20

data:
0
75
100
75
0
@/chart

## 6. Optimisation: The Open Box

[example]
Squares of side x are cut from the corners of a 20 cm × 20 cm sheet,
and the flaps folded up to form an open box. Maximise the volume.
Step 1: the base is 20 − 2x on each side, the depth is x, so

@math label="eq:box-volume"
V(x) = x(20 - 2x)^2
@/math

Step 2: expand: V(x) = 400x − 80x² + 4x³.
Step 3: V′(x) = 400 − 160x + 12x² = 4(3x − 10)(x − 10), so the
critical points are x = 10/3 and x = 10.
Step 4: x = 10 gives V = 0 (an empty box) — reject it; and
V″(10/3) = −160 + 24 × 10/3 = −80 < 0, confirming a maximum.
Step 5: cut 10/3 ≈ 3.33 cm squares, giving
V = (10/3)(40/3)² = 16000/27 ≈ 592.6 cm³.
[/example]

[tip]
In box problems the sensible range of x is 0 < x < half the sheet side;
restricting to that window instantly discards roots like x = 10 that
are mathematically valid but physically useless.
[/tip]

## 7. Area Under a Curve

Integration adds up infinitely many thin rectangles of width dx:

@math label="eq:definite-integral"
\int_0^3 x^2 \, dx = 9
@/math

[example]
Area under y = x² from x = 0 to x = 3.
Step 1: the antiderivative of x² is x³/3.
Step 2: evaluate: 3³/3 − 0³/3 = 27/3 = 9 square units.
[/example]

Cross-check with a straight line: y = 2x from 0 to 4 encloses a
triangle of base 4 and height 8. The integral gives 16, and so does
½ × 4 × 8 — integration agrees exactly with geometry wherever
geometry can reach.

[tip]
For straight-line boundaries, compute the area with triangles and
trapeziums from coordinate geometry — then use the integral as a
check. For curved boundaries, the integral is the only tool that
works.
[/tip]

## 8. Between Curves and Moving Objects

When one curve sits above another, subtract before integrating:

@math label="eq:area-between"
\int_0^1 (x - x^2) \, dx = \frac{1}{2} - \frac{1}{3} = \frac{1}{6}
@/math

That is the area trapped between y = x and y = x² over the first unit —
a region no ruler could measure.

[example]
A particle moves with velocity v(t) = 3t². How far does it travel from
t = 0 to t = 2?
Step 1: displacement is the integral of velocity:
∫ from 0 to 2 of 3t² dt = t³ evaluated from 0 to 2 = 8.
Step 2: the particle covers 8 m. If the velocity changed sign, the
distance travelled would need |v|, while the displacement uses v —
motion in opposite directions cancels in displacement only.
[/example]

[info]
The same integral that prices a fence also drives engineering: beam
deflection, battery charge, drug dosage, and probability densities are
all areas under curves — calculus is the universal accounting system
for accumulating change.
[/info]

## 9. Quick Revision

| Problem | Method | Answer |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Minimise x² − 8x + 7 | first derivative test | minimum at x = 4, value −9 |
| Classify 2x³ − 9x² + 12x | second derivative test | max 5 at x = 1, min 4 at x = 2 |
| Extremes of x³ − 3x on −2 to 2 | check critical points and endpoints | max 2, min −2 |
| Largest pen from 40 m of fence | A = x(20 − x), A′ = 0 | 10 m × 10 m, 100 m² |
| Open box from a 20 cm sheet | V = x(20 − 2x)² | cut 10/3 cm, V ≈ 592.6 cm³ |
| Area under x² from 0 to 3 | antiderivative x³/3 | 9 |
| Area between y = x and y = x², 0 to 1 | subtract then integrate | 1/6 |
| Displacement for v = 3t², 0 to 2 | integrate velocity | 8 m |

[summary]
Find critical points with f′, classify them with f″, remember the
endpoints, and answer optimisation questions in context with units;
then let the definite integral measure what geometry cannot — areas
under and between curves, and the distance hidden inside a velocity.
[/summary]
